on-arrival: freikorps_strength += 15

= The Annaberg

In the plebiscite of 20 March Upper Silesia voted six to four to stay German, the towns for Germany and many of the mining villages for Poland. On the night of 2 May Wojciech Korfanty's insurgents rise and take most of the industrial district. Berlin forbids any German counter-attack in public and lets the Freikorps go in private.

On 21 May the Bavarian Freikorps Oberland storms the Annaberg, a hill with a monastery on top. The nationalist papers are full of heroes, and recruiting offices open in every city.

- @expose: Print the names of the men who pay the Freikorps.
- @fraternise: Call on the miners of both nations to put down their guns.

@expose
subtitle: Their offices will be wrecked in the night, and the police will know where to look for the men who did it.
on-arrival: freikorps_strength -= 8; repression += 3; lean_offensive += 1

The Rote Fahne prints the names of the industrialists who fund the Freikorps and the addresses of their recruiting offices. Several offices are wrecked before the week is out.

@fraternise
subtitle: Right, and unpopular for now.
on-arrival: worker_support -= 1; lean_luxemburgist += 1; lean_unionist += 1

Our leaflet goes out in German and Polish: the coal belongs to those who dig it. In a few pits, miners of both languages hold a joint meeting. Then the fighting reaches them.
